    "question": "How many elements are there in the power set of a set that has exactly 5 distinct elements?",
    "options": {
      "A": "5",
      "B": "10",
      "C": "25",
      "D": "32"
    },
    "correct_answer": "D",
    "explanation": "The power set of a set with n elements contains 2ⁿ subsets. For n = 5, 2⁵ = 32.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "For functions f : A → B and g : B → C, which statement about the composition g∘f is always true?",
    "options": {
      "A": "If f is injective, then g∘f is injective.",
      "B": "If g is surjective, then g∘f is surjective.",
      "C": "If f is bijective, then g∘f is bijective.",
      "D": "If g∘f is injective, then f is injective."
    },
    "correct_answer": "D",
    "explanation": "If the composition g∘f maps distinct elements of A to distinct elements of C, then f cannot map two different elements of A to the same element of B; hence f must be injective. The other implications are not guaranteed.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  },
  {
    "question": "Define a relation R on the set of all integers by a R b ⇔ a − b is divisible by 5. Which properties does R satisfy?",
    "options": {
      "A": "Reflexive only",
      "B": "Symmetric and transitive only",
      "C": "Reflexive, symmetric, and transitive (equivalence relation)",
      "D": "None of the above"
    },
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "explanation": "For any integer a, a−a = 0 is divisible by 5, so R is reflexive. If a−b is divisible by 5, then b−a = -(a−b) is also divisible by 5, giving symmetry. If a−b and b−c are each divisible by 5, then a−c = (a−b)+(b−c) is divisible by 5, giving transitivity. Hence R is an equivalence relation.",
    "difficulty": "Medium"
  }
